## 194 The aging impact on cardiac mesenchymal like stromal cells $(\pmb{\mathsf{S}}+\pmb{\mathsf{P}}+)$ 1
H. Martini; D. Maggiorani; L. Lefevre; M. Dutaur; J. Mialet-Perez; A. Parini; D. Cussac; V. Douin-Echinard Institute of Cardiovascular and Metabolic Diseases, Toulouse, France
Ageing is associated with progressive modifications of heart structure and functions, and is a dominant risk factor for developing cardiovascular diseases. In general, the accumulation of senescent cells within a tissue is thought to drive age-related organ dysfunction. Concerning the heart, it has been recently shown that cardiomyocytes biology and function are altered during ageing. In addition to cardiomyocytes, the heart contains mesenchymal-like stromal cells $(\mathsf{S}+\mathsf{P}+)$ that participate in regulation of cardiac homeostasis through the differentiation into vascular cells and their paracrine activity. In the present study, we investigated the effects of ageing on the phenotype and function of cardiac mesenchymal-like stromal cells $(\mathsf{S}+\mathsf{P}+)$ . Cardiac mesenchymal-like stromal cells $(\mathsf{S}+\mathsf{P}+)$ from young (3 months) and old (20 months) $C5781/6$ mice were isolated by enzymatic digestion of hearts and sorted by flow cytometry based on sca- $1+{\mathsf{P D G F R}}\alpha+$ co-expression and the lack of endothelial (CD31-) and hematopoietic (CD45-) markers. During physiological ageing, ${\mathsf{S}}{+}{\mathsf{P}}{+}$ cell population acquired a senescence associated secretory phenotype (SASP) characterized by increased gene expression of inflammatory mediators (Ccl2), growth factors $(\mathsf{l g}^{\mathsf{f}}\mathsf{1})$ and pro-angiogenic factors (Vegfa). In addition, we demonstrated, by immunofluorescence and gene expression analysis, that ageing modified the differentiation pathways of $\mathsf{S}{+}\mathsf{P}{+}$ cells with decreased endothelial and increased smooth muscle cell differentiation potentials. These modifications were associated with specific modulations of cell cycle-related senescence program (p15, p16, p18). To understand how oxidative damages could trigger a senescence program in ${\mathsf{S}}{+}{\mathsf{P}}{+}$ cells, we used a transgenic mice model with specific cardiomyocyte expression of the hydrogen peroxide producing enzyme MonoAmine Oxydase A. Gene expression and flow cytometry phenotype analyses showed that, in these mice, ${\mathsf{S}}{+}{\mathsf{P}}{+}$ cells underwent a senescent program and phenotype modifications similar to those observed in aged animals. These results suggest that oxidative stress may be one of the factors inducing senescence of ${\mathsf{S}}{+}{\mathsf{P}}{+}$ cardiac stromal cells. In conclusion, our results show that ageing induces significant changes in lineage and secretory properties of $\mathsf{S}{+}\mathsf{P}{+}$ cells that could play a role in age-associated cardiac dysfunction.
## 195 Reversal of premature aging markers after bariatric surgery
P J. Hohensinner1; B. Ebenbauer1; C. Kaun1; M. Prager2; J. Wojta1; G. Rega-Kaun1 1Medical University of Vienna, Cardiology, Vienna, Austria; 2Hietzing Hospital, Vienna, Austria
Background: Obesity is considered to be a major risk factor in developing cardiac disease. In addition, obese patients suffer from a premature aging phenotype including increased secretion of senescence associated secretory proteins (SASP) and reduced telomere length compared to healthy controls.
Purpose: The aim of our study was to determine, if bariatric surgery and the resulting weight loss could reverse the previously observed premature aging phenotype.
Methods: We enrolled 76 patients undergoing bariatric surgery. Blood samples were taken before and 12 months after surgery. Markers of premature aging including the SASP IL6 and PAI-1 as well as telomere length and telomere oxidation were evaluated.
Results: Overall, patients showed a significant drop of body mass index $(44.5\pm4.2\$ before surgery versus $27.5\pm3.6$ after surgery, $\mathsf{p}{<}0.001$ ). In addition plasma levels for IL6 $(3.1\pm2.4\mathrm{\pg/ml}$ before versus $1.7\pm1.5~\mathsf{p g}/\mathsf{m l}$ after bariatric surgery, $\mathsf{p}{<}0.001$ ) and PAI-1 $(98.4\pm17.2\mathrm{ng/ml}$ before versus $83.8\pm27.1{\mathrm{ng/ml}}$ after bariatric surgery, $\mathsf{p}{<}0.001$ ) were significantly reduced after surgery. Telomere length on average increased by $58\%$ in the patient cohort $(0.37\pm0.28\$ a.u. before versus $0.59\pm0.28$ a.u. after bariatric surgery, $\mathsf{p}{<}0.001$ ). The telomere increase was accompanied by a reduction in the telomere oxidation index $2.86\pm4.4$ before versus $0.78\pm0.56$ after bariatric surgery, $\mathsf{p}{<}0.001$ ) indicating reduced oxidative stress for the telomeric region. This is further supported by an inverse correlation of telomere length with telomere oxidation at both time points $r=-0.376$ , $\mathsf{p}{<}0.001$ pre surgery and $\mathrm{r}{=}{-}0.705$ , $\mathsf{p}{<}0.001$ post surgery).
Conclusion: Our data indicate a significant reduction of the SASP IL6 and PAI-1 in plasma 12 months after bariatric surgery. In addition we observed an increase in telomere length in this setting. However, given the reduction in oxidative stress at telomeric regions we speculate that the increased telomere length is not due to active elongation but due to reduced breakage caused by telomere oxidation. Overall, bariatric surgery ameliorated the premature aging phenotype of previously morbidly obese patients.
